#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158396} :activity/id #uuid "97fd4430-aac7-55ae-afde-da3c6748fa89" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050309 :topic/id #uuid "3ece60c2-2440-5884-ade0-5ec738e1db78" :topic/math-academy-id 2819} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158469 :tutorial/content "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nwe know that if $A$ has distinct eigenvalues, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nThis formula works even when the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2$ are complex. However, using it directly requires working with complex exponentials, which can be cumbersome.\n\nA more direct method is to find just *one* of the complex solutions, say $\\mathbf x_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}_1 e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t},$ and separate it into its *real and imaginary parts*.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t} \\\\  & ={{{w}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Real Part}}\\,+\\,\\text{i}\\,\\,\\,{{{w}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Imaginary Part}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general *real-valued* solution is then a linear combination of these two parts:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x(t) = c_1 \\mathbf w_1(t) + c_2 \\mathbf w_2(t)\n$$\n\n**Note:** This method may seem surprising. Why can we construct the general solution using only *one* of the complex solutions, $\\mathbf x_1(t)?$\n\nThe reason is that the second solution, $\\mathbf x_2(t),$ doesn't provide any new information. For a real matrix $A,$ the complex eigenvalues and eigenvectors come in *conjugate pairs*, which means $\\mathbf x_2(t)$ is simply the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf x_1(t).$\n\nSince $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf w_1 (t) - \\textrm{i} \\, \\mathbf w_2(t),$ it is also a linear combination of the real and imaginary parts of $\\mathbf x_1(t).$\n\nTo build our general solution, we just need two *linearly independent* real solutions. The key facts are the following:\n\n- For a system with complex eigenvalues, the real part $\\mathbf w_1(t)$ and the imaginary part $\\mathbf w_2(t)$ are *always* linearly independent.\n\n- Since they are two linearly independent solutions, they can form the basis for the general solution.\n\nSo, we can use them directly and bypass the need to work with $\\mathbf x_2(t)$ at all.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a93ff6d6-1eb0-5e42-8b69-ae73fc891839" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4283 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "24b6ab49-7a81-5a3b-a140-aabc60a9f348" :step/math-academy-id 577657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158397}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157069 :question/id #uuid "2a284986-5300-5bad-a33c-aa2bc47712b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7043"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414138}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "33bad08d-6b45-5628-aba5-8acb1bac5825" :step/math-academy-id 577658 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158398}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157070 :question/id #uuid "8e797f4b-fe53-5d0a-a4b0-8fb51ee04c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-7044"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :k